{"decision":"promote","rationale":"I recommend promoting, but it is a close call. No contract violation is shown. Integrity checks are clean in all 28 cases: address_mismatch_pairs=0, model admissions_without_recorded_latency=0, and coverage moves by at most ~40 reads per case. The source differs from the incumbent in three ways. The new drain_pending=0.5 and pending_cap=1 are declared in range. Two options the incumbent never used (drain_block=2, pending_scope) are removed. One cleanup step is moved earlier, which changes no behaviour. The new mechanism uses only the model's own booked reads, a recent average of the write share, and hardware timings. It changes only how many buffered writes a forced drain books at the next read's admission; issued predictions are never revised. On the primary objectives, training improves clearly. Mean training core error falls from 0.1164% to 0.0965% (-0.020 pp, -17.1%). Training request MAE/L falls from 0.08630 to 0.08484 (-1.7%), and 6 of the 7 changed training cases improve MAE/L. The core-error gain comes from lbm, the worst training case: 0.718% to 0.431% (-0.29 pp, -40%). The request level confirms it: lbm MAE/L -1.7% and reads above 1L -1.9%, with coverage unchanged, so this is not cancellation or lost samples. gap_sssp's tail improves materially: 5L count -8.4% and tail metric -9.0%. Validation neither confirms nor contradicts the gain. Core error changes by +0.0002 pp (+0.04%) and MAE/L by +0.08%; only 4 of 14 cases change, and the dominant val7 failure (-5.05%) is untouched. Over all 28 cases weighted equally, core error falls 3.4% and MAE/L falls 1.0%. The cost is a shift in severe tails. Typical errors fall (reads above 1L: training -2.2%, validation -0.1%), but reads above 5L rise in most changed cases. Across training and validation the 5L count is roughly flat (-110 in training, +103 in validation). The physical idea is plausible: fills during a forced drain evict writebacks that lengthen the drain. Unlike the rejected round-5 setting (drain_pending=1), this version improves lbm's request-level accuracy along with its core error. I judge the primary-metric gain to outweigh small, low-frequency tail regressions, but the gain is concentrated in one case and validation does not reproduce it.","improvements":["Training mean core error: 0.1164% to 0.0965% (-0.0199 pp, -17.1%). The signed mean falls from +0.088% to +0.067%, so absolute error drops without sign cancellation.","lbm (worst training case): core error 0.718% to 0.431% (-0.287 pp, -40%). MAE/L 0.5047 to 0.4961 (-1.7%). Reads above 1L 200,613 to 196,765 (-1.9%). Matched-read coverage is essentially unchanged (+41 reads).","Training mean request MAE/L: 0.08630 to 0.08484 (-1.7%). 6 of the 7 changed cases improve: gap_sssp -5.5%, arizona -2.6%, charlie -1.4%, lbm -1.7%, gap_bfs -0.6%, whiskey -0.5%. bravo is flat (+0.01%).","gap_sssp tail: reads above 5L 2,408 to 2,205 (-8.4%); tail metric -9.0%; reads above 1L -9.0%; P99 178 to 162 cycles.","Training reads above 1L: 226,149 to 221,220 (-2.2%). Excluding lbm: 25,536 to 24,455 (-4.2%). Training reads above 5L: 4,191 to 4,081 (-2.6%). Training tail metric -0.4%.","arizona P99.9/L 6.65 to 6.46 (-2.9%). whiskey P99.9/L 6.50 to 6.37 (-2.0%). charlie's largest overprediction 1,424 to 1,083 cycles.","val11: MAE/L -1.8%, tail metric -4.2%, P99 95 to 91 cycles.","All 28 cases weighted equally: core error 0.2871% to 0.2772% (-3.4%); MAE/L 0.07169 to 0.07098 (-1.0%).","Against the stage-entry FixedLat reference, all headline metrics are retained or improved. Training: core error 23.67% to 0.0965% (-99.6%), MAE/L 0.5425 to 0.0848 (-84%), P99.9/L 4.38 to 3.16, reads above 5L 6,445 to 4,081. Validation: core error 24.90% to 0.458% (-98.2%), MAE/L 0.531 to 0.0571 (-89%), P99.9/L 3.49 to 2.39, reads above 5L 3,100 to 1,743. This is the single-core stage, so all of these are single-core measurements."],"accepted_regressions":["Validation means are flat to slightly worse. Core error is 0.45774% to 0.45792% (+0.0002 pp, +0.04%). MAE/L is 0.05707 to 0.05712 (+0.08%). Abs drift/L is +0.2%.","Validation severe tails worsen. Reads above 5L go from 1,640 to 1,743 (+103, +6.3%), with val14 accounting for +90 (441 to 531, +20%). Validation P99.9/L goes from 2.345 to 2.393 (+2.0%). These are rare events (about 0.1% of reads or fewer), and reads above 1L fall slightly.","val8 regresses on every measure. Core error +0.0014 pp. MAE/L +1.7%. P99.9/L 3.45 to 3.84 (+11.3%, 360 to 401 cycles). Reads above 5L 134 to 148.","val14: MAE/L +1.2% and P99.9/L +2.1%, though core error improves slightly (0.0722% to 0.0716%). val11: core error +0.0017 pp and P99.9/L +2.5%, despite better MAE/L.","Reads above 5L rise in most changed training cases except gap_sssp. lbm +9, arizona +24, charlie +11, gap_bfs +45 (+8%), bravo +7. Training 5L excluding gap_sssp: 1,783 to 1,876 (+5.2%).","lbm tail: P99.9/L +1.3%, tail metric +2.0%, P99 589 to 601 cycles, largest over/under-prediction +1,335/-1,955 vs +1,178/-1,869 cycles.","lbm abs drift/L 0.0001 to 0.0025. Still small, and the MAE/L improvement shows the core gain is not cancellation.","Training P99.9/L +0.4% (3.148 to 3.160). gap_sssp P99.9/L +3.2% and gap_bfs P99.9/L +3.2%, though gap_sssp's 5L count falls.","Excluding lbm, training mean core error is flat to slightly worse: 0.07017% to 0.07079% (+0.0006 pp, +0.9%). charlie is +0.005 pp and arizona +0.004 pp."],"contract_findings":["Mechanical integrity is clean. address_mismatch_pairs=0 and model admissions_without_recorded_latency=0 in all 28 cases. Coverage changes are negligible (at most about ±40 matched reads per case). lbm's one-read difference between model and oracle admissions comes from the closed-loop simulation, not a matching failure.","Parameter declarations are valid. drain_pending=0.5 is declared with range [0,2]; pending_cap=1 with range [0,1]. All other parameters are identical to the incumbent's.","The source diff is limited to three changes, none adding hidden behaviour. (1) drain_block option 2 and pending_scope are removed; the incumbent never used either (drain_block=1, pending_scope defaulted to 0). (2) The pruning of outstanding reads now runs before the drain. This is equivalent because pending_writebacks already filters on r.done > t. (3) drain_pending is now active.","Predictions are still made once at admission and never revised. The drain_pending mechanism uses only the model's own booked reads (queue-departure and predicted-completion times), a 256-admission running average of the write share, and hardware timing parameters. It changes only the number of writes booked into a forced drain.","No use of future requests, oracle completions, workload names, addresses (beyond same-line forwarding, unchanged from the incumbent) or evaluation identities was found. No internal command scheduler was added; the reservation timeline structure is unchanged."],"contract_violation":false,"uncertainty":["The training core-error gain rests on a single case: lbm accounts for essentially all of it, and without lbm training core error is +0.9% worse. The validation core error does not replicate it (+0.04%), so the gain may not generalise beyond write-heavy streaming workloads like lbm.","The value 0.5 was chosen after drain_pending=1 was rejected in round 5. With so few settings tried, part of the lbm gain may come from tuning to lbm rather than from better physics.","Severe errors are redistributed rather than removed. Reads above 1L fall broadly, but reads above 5L rise in 7 of the 10 changed cases, and validation 5L rises 6.3%. The fixed writes-per-read estimate may overestimate drain length in some phases, pushing later reads into extreme overprediction.","The largest remaining validation failure (val7, -5.05% core error) is untouched. Validation mean core error (0.458%) is still dominated by it, so this round leaves the most serious generalisation weakness unaddressed.","Each case is a single run. Several of the per-case core-error differences (about 0.001–0.005 pp) are small enough that they may not be meaningful.","The candidate's own explanation of drain physics (the discount on fills that write back before the drain ends, and the pipeline-depth cap) was treated as unverified. The model reads nothing that would show whether those writebacks actually arrive during the drain. Synthetic and replay diagnostics were not used as decision evidence."]}
